              {
                "text": "I'm mapping the grid's lattice of 5s forming cells, and noticing a 3 marker at one corner near row14, col4. The output turns the cell bounded by rows 8-11, cols 1-4 into a rectangle outline of 3s, while the original 3 marker reverts to a 5 \u2014 I'm trying to figure out why that particular cell was selected.\n\nChecking the walls of that cell, I find 7s occupy the top, left, right, and bottom edges connecting the corner 5s \u2014 it's fully enclosed on all four sides. So the rule seems to be: locate the cell whose perimeter is completely walled with 7s, draw its rectangle outline in 3, remove those 7s, and turn the standalone 3 marker back into a 5, leaving everything else untouched. I feel confident enough in this pattern to test it.",
